DETAILED EXPLANATION OF MY PROBLEM STATEMENT:

Problem Statement: AETHER – AI-Powered Industrial Energy Intelligence and Cost Reduction System

Industrial facilities consume substantial amounts of electricity to operate machinery, production lines, compressors, chillers, and other equipment. However, many facilities lack an integrated, intelligent system to continuously analyse machine-level energy consumption, anticipate peak electricity demand, identify abnormal usage patterns, and recommend cost-effective operational improvements.

Conventional energy monitoring approaches often focus on displaying historical consumption and generating periodic reports. This makes it difficult for facility operators to identify inefficient energy usage in time, predict upcoming demand spikes, investigate unusual consumption, and take informed decisions to reduce electricity costs without disrupting production.

The absence of a unified, data-driven decision-support system creates a gap between collecting energy data and converting it into actionable operational insights.

**The problem is to develop an AI-powered Industrial Energy Management System that transforms energy consumption data into predictive insights and actionable recommendations.** The system should integrate energy data processing, machine-level analytics, demand forecasting, anomaly detection, and intelligent rule-based recommendations to help industrial operators identify potential inefficiencies and evaluate energy-saving opportunities.

The proposed solution, **AETHER**, aims to provide a unified platform that monitors energy consumption, predicts future demand using machine learning, detects unusual energy patterns, recommends peak-load optimisation strategies, and evaluates potential savings through a human-approved simulation and measurement-and-verification workflow.

The ultimate objective is to help industries move from reactive energy monitoring towards proactive, data-driven energy management, supporting improved operational efficiency, informed decision-making, and potential reductions in energy costs.
